'''Zong''' ([[Giokji]]: 總; [[POJ]]: ''Chóng''), officially the State of Zong ([[Giokji]]: 總國; [[POJ]]: ''Chóngkok''), is a [[w:Sovereign state|sovereign state]] in [[Eastern Fosia]]. It is situated along the eastern seaboard of the [[Fosia|Fosian]] continent, bordering the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of over 393 million people, it is one of the most populous and economically significant nations in Adonia. Zong operates as a unitary parliamentary constitutional monarchy under King [[Kengjun]] and Prime Minister [[Tan Bopo]], with its capital and largest city located at [[Huwei]]. The nation possesses a deep and complex history spanning thousands of years, characterized by ancient philosophical traditions, maritime expansion, and a unique transition from imperial absolutism to a modern democratic state.

[[File:Zong_Capital_Cityscape.jpg|thumb|right|The modern skyline of Huwei, showcasing the economic miracle of the post-war era alongside ancient architectural elements.]]

==History==
The Imperial and Revolutionary History of Zong encompasses a comprehensive record from foundational antiquity to its modern incarnation as a global powerhouse.

===Etymology===
The name of the state derives from the ancient character for gather or total, which was historically utilized by early tribes in the [[Jade Peninsula]] to describe the unification of the diverse river valley settlements. During the classical era, the term became associated with the centralized authority of the imperial courts. In regional languages such as Shansi Cantonese, the name is pronounced as Zung, while in the widespread Hokkien dialects it is pronounced as Chóng.

===Prehistory===
Human habitation in the region dates back to the [[w:Paleolithic|Paleolithic]] era, with archaeological evidence suggesting early hominid settlements along the fertile basins of the Jade River and the Pearl River. The earliest agrarian societies emerged around 5000 BCE, cultivating rice and millet. These early settlements eventually coalesced into advanced regional cultures, such as the Nakashima Culture in the south and the early animist tribes of the north. The invention of the [[Donghai Script]] around the Jade Peninsula marked the transition from prehistory into recorded antiquity, paving the way for the region's first organized polities.

===Foundational Antiquity===
The genesis of East Fosian civilization is inextricably linked to the administrative and cultural hegemony of Zong. During foundational antiquity, Zong functioned as the primary progenitor for the region, establishing the legal and linguistic blueprint that allowed neighboring maritime polities to transition from oral tribalism to structured statehood. The codification of the Zong Scrolls and the Old Zong Language provided a cohesive framework for governance across the Shendan Ocean basin. 

However, Zong’s role was not merely one of passive cultural export. The archival record confirms that Zong’s shift from cultural progenitor to regional hegemon was cemented by military force, most notably the 285 CE invasion of the [[Kingdom of Donghai]]. This terrestrial conquest was inadvertently aided by the [[Haizei]], a mysterious confederation of maritime raiders from the islands of Yanbaru and Hapo who simultaneously devastated Donghai's northern coastal defenses. This dual assault ended a millennium-long lineage and demonstrated that Zong script and law would be followed by Zong steel, marking the transition from a terrestrial entity to a continental empire. 

[[File:Donghai_Artifacts.jpg|thumb|left|Artifacts recovered from the ancient Kingdom of Donghai, demonstrating early Fosian maritime craftsmanship prior to the Zong conquest.]]

The cultural exports of early Zong profoundly impacted regional development. The dissemination of Kanji and the Zong Scrolls provided the vital administrative machinery required for tax-allotment and legal codification in neighboring maritime states like [[Okaiken]], allowing them to end their silent eras and replace fragmented oral histories with verifiable written law. Furthermore, the export of Zong philosophical texts and epic dynastic sagas established the theological and moral justification for divine mandates of rule among regional aristocracies. Through the introduction of administrative meritocracy—systems of governance based on bureaucratic rank rather than hereditary status—foreign courts were enabled to dismantle fractured tribal clan systems in favor of stabilized, stratified bureaucracies.

===The Sui Dynasty and the Era of High Continental Diplomacy===
The Sui Dynasty represented the zenith of Zong’s architectural rigor and administrative centralization. During this era, the Zong capital served as a celestial model for the Fosian world, defined by a geometric precision that foreign courts viewed as the ultimate manifestation of civilizational order. This High Continental model directly influenced the grid-based urban planning of foreign capitals like Heishu-kyō. However, this centralization was not universally accepted; while northern Sanese elites embraced the Sui model, southern Umijin maritime clans offered fierce resistance, maintaining an ocean-oriented autonomy that highlighted the friction between Zong-style terrestrial rigidity and Fosian maritime tradition.

During the medieval period, the eastern maritime frontiers were characterized by shifting powers. The rugged coastal archipelago in the northeast, known as [[Mabatok]], fell under the control of the foreign [[Kingdom of Kabisayaan]]. Ruled by maritime sea-lords, Mabatok became a hybrid cultural zone where indigenous populations were subjected to an aristocratic minority practicing [[Anitism]]. This occupation lasted nearly two centuries before the resurgent Zong military finally integrated the archipelago, absorbing advanced naval technologies that would later fuel its own imperial expansion. 

[[File:Zong_Sui_Architecture.jpg|thumb|right|Traditional Sui-era architecture, characterized by tiled roofing and complex timber-joinery that heavily influenced regional capitals.]]

The strategic importance of the Zong-Sanese embassies during this period was paramount. These missions facilitated the export of the Twelve Level Rank system, a meritocratic bureaucracy that replaced hereditary titles with a centralized civil service. By exporting these models, Zong stabilized foreign courts, transforming them from loose confederations into managed imperial states. Technological transfers heavily influenced the continent. In medicine, the dissemination of advanced anatomical and herbal knowledge provided a scientific basis for regional healthcare. In astronomy, the export of celestial mapping and calendar systems proved critical for both state ritual and agricultural synchronization.

===The Jade Sea War and the Triple Kingdoms Alliance===
The 14th century marked a profound strategic pivot as the [[Jade Sea War]] forced Zong to transition from a land-focused power to a maritime-dominant empire. This conflict was an existential struggle that reshaped the geopolitical architecture of Fosia. Facing a brutal land blockade by the [[Kingdom of Daras]], Zong was forced to abandon its terrestrial focus to secure the flow of trade and resources.

This war necessitated the formation of the Triple Kingdoms Alliance, a defensive coalition comprising the Kingdom of Zong and its allies, [[Fenia]] and Shanjian. The conflict was defined by highly contrasting strategic approaches. The Darasian offensive initiated a devastating land-based blockade, severing Zong's overland trade routes to the west and strangulating the continental economy. In response, the Triple Kingdoms Alliance dramatically redirected its resources to maritime defense, creating a massive naval infrastructure to break the chokehold. This strategy proved immensely successful, culminating in the Heping Settlement of 1390. This treaty formalized the Triple Kingdoms’ victory, secured a long-term Fosian peace, and unequivocally established Zong as an oceanic hegemon.

[[File:Triple_Kingdoms_Fleet.png|thumb|left|A historical reconstruction of the Triple Kingdoms Alliance naval fleet preparing for engagement during the Jade Sea War.]]

===The Shendan Expansion and New World Colonization===
Zong’s maritime doctrine necessitated the annexation of new trade routes to bypass the legacy of the Darasian land blockades. This Shendan Expansion saw Zong evolve from a coastal defender into a global pioneer. The multi-crew war canoes and outrigger influences of the earlier Haizei raids and the Mabatok era were refined into massive oceanic fleets capable of trans-oceanic voyages.

Zong spearheaded the discovery and colonization of the [[New World]], establishing the Tamsui colony as its primary colonial hub on the continent of [[Lurandia]]. Tamsui served as a critical nexus for the Fosian diaspora, integrating displaced merchants and artisans into a thriving colonial economy. Concurrently, the establishment of the colony of [[Sinchew]] introduced a darker chapter of imperial history. To fuel its massive plantation economy, a Zong merchant named [[Ong Sam]] initiated the illicit [[Secret Trade]] in the 1580s. Utilizing pirates and mercenaries from Yanbaru and Mitoyagi, this system kidnapped peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]], transporting them to Lurandia as enslaved labor. Because the Zongese Edict of 1500 officially banned slavery on the mainland, the trade operated in a legal gray area overseas, leaving a profound demographic legacy on the colonial populations that remains a sensitive historiographical subject today.

[[File:Haihia_Treaty_Signing.jpg|thumb|right|An illustration of diplomats Lim Ming and Chen An finalizing the terms of the An-Ming Understanding in 1605.]]

To manage overlapping colonial spheres and mitigate conflict with expanding [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] powers and the [[Sultanate of Maros]], Zong diplomat [[Lim Ming]] and envoy [[Chen An]] negotiated the [[An-Ming Understanding]] between 1605 and 1606. Signed in the port city of Haihia, these conventions formally partitioned the continents of Lurandia and [[Abyala]] into respective Zong and Marosan spheres of influence, establishing a legal framework that proved Eastern Fosian states could compete with Western colonial empires on equal footing.

===Crisis of the 19th Century===
The 19th century brought an end to Zong’s continental isolation as the Western Iron Envoys challenged the Fosian order. These Illypnian powers imposed unequal treaties that stripped away tariff autonomy, forcing Zong to modernize its diplomatic and military apparatus. Rather than suffering absolute collapse, progressive factions initiated a transition to a constitutional monarchy in 1827. In response to Western threats, Zong formalized a defensive coalition through the Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding in 1895. This agreement signaled a shift from regional competition to cooperative defense, establishing coordinated naval patrols to protect Fosian sovereignty.

During the upheavals of the Sengoku era in the Sanese archipelago, Zong maintained a policy of strategic tolerance toward displaced Sanese ronin and artisans. Tamsui became a safe harbor for these refugees, creating a unique cross-cultural military-merchant class. This diaspora community acted as a permanent, pro-Zong lobby within the Sanese social structure, facilitating the rapid transfer of industrial knowledge and administrative techniques that would prove vital during the modernization efforts of the late 19th century.

===Modern Zong===
Zong’s participation in the [[Great Adonian War]] was framed as a defense of Fosian resource sovereignty. The conflict was a devastating maritime struggle that exhausted the imperial system, leading to a profound internal political restructuring. The aftermath of the war saw the loss of traditional colonial holdings and the formation of a transitional republic that eventually stabilized into the highly robust unitary parliamentary constitutional monarchy present today.

In the modern era, Zong has transitioned into an industrial titan, emphasizing maritime dominance and industrial partnership over territorial expansion. It remains the Brain of Fosia; from its ancient role as the progenitor of script and law to its modern status as a digital and industrial hegemon, Zong continues to exert a profound and enduring influence on the civilizational landscape of the Shendan Ocean.

==Geography==
===Physical Characteristics===
Zong occupies a vast and topographically diverse landmass in Eastern Fosia. The geography is broadly divided into the rugged, mountainous regions of the west and the fertile, densely populated river deltas of the east. The Jade Peninsula juts out into the Shendan Ocean, forming a natural maritime gateway that has dictated the nation's naval history. The eastern seaboard is characterized by deep-water ports and intricate archipelagos.

[[File:Zong_Karst_Mountains.jpg|thumb|right|The breathtaking karst mountain landscapes of the western interior, known for their mist-shrouded peaks and deep river valleys.]]

===Climate===
The country experiences a wide range of climatic conditions due to its vast latitudinal extent. The northern territories experience a [[w:Humid continental climate|humid continental climate]] with freezing winters and warm summers. The central river valleys feature a temperate climate, while the southern coastal regions, including the Cantonese-speaking hubs of San-wui and Tyun-mun, are defined by a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] subject to seasonal monsoons and occasional typhoons.

